# Episode 3: The Vasa. (shot, chapter-or-None, narration)
SCRIPT = [
 ("harbour_sail", None, "On the tenth of August, sixteen twenty-eight, the most powerful warship Sweden had ever built set sail from Stockholm. Crowds lined the shore. Foreign ambassadors were watching. The guns were run out to fire a salute."),
 ("sinking", None, "The ship sailed about thirteen hundred metres. Then a gust of wind caught her sails, water poured in through the open gunports, and in front of the whole city, the Vasa sank."),
 ("title", None, "This is the story of the Vasa. The mighty warship that didn't even make it out of the harbour, and how, three hundred and thirty-three years later, it came back."),

 ("king", "The King's Navy", "In the early sixteen hundreds, Sweden was on the rise. Under King Gustavus Adolphus, it was becoming the dominant power around the Baltic Sea. Historians call this period Sweden's Age of Greatness."),
 ("king", None, "Gustavus Adolphus was a soldier king. Energetic, ambitious, and very hands-on, he reformed Sweden's army and led it himself. He took a close personal interest in his warships too, down to how many guns they would carry. That detail matters, as we'll see."),
 ("navy", None, "But greatness needs a navy. From sixteen twenty-one, Sweden was at war with Poland-Lithuania, and the king's plans depended on controlling the Baltic. Then, in sixteen twenty-five, a storm in the Bay of Riga wrecked ten Swedish ships. The navy needed new warships, and it needed them fast."),

 ("shipyard", "Building a Giant", "On the sixteenth of January, sixteen twenty-five, the Dutch shipwright Henrik Hybertsson, known as Master Henrik, and his partner Arendt de Groote signed a contract to build four ships for the Swedish crown. Two small, and two large. The first of the large ships would become the Vasa."),
 ("shipyard", None, "Timber came from Swedish estates, and from as far away as Riga, Königsberg and Amsterdam. Early in sixteen twenty-six, the keel was laid. But Master Henrik fell ill, and supervision passed to another shipwright, Henrik Jacobsson, known as Hein."),
 ("plans", None, "Then the king made a fateful request. In August sixteen twenty-six, he ordered seventy-two heavy twenty-four-pound guns for the ship. That was far too many for a single gun deck. So the Vasa got two full gun decks, stacked one above the other. Very few ships of the time were built that way."),
 ("plans", None, "All that heavy metal sat high above the waterline. And the ship's builders didn't have the tools to calculate stability that engineers use today. Shipbuilding was done largely by experience and rules of thumb. For a ship this unusual, experience wasn't much help."),
 ("carvings", None, "The Vasa was also meant to impress. Nearly five hundred carved sculptures covered the ship, mostly on the towering stern and the bow, painted in bright colours. This wasn't just a warship. It was a floating statement of the king's power."),
 ("shipyard", None, "Master Henrik didn't live to see her finished. He died in the spring of sixteen twenty-seven. The ship was launched that same spring. Fully rigged, she would stretch to around sixty-nine metres, weigh more than twelve hundred tonnes, and carry a crew of one hundred and forty-five sailors, plus three hundred soldiers."),

 ("test", "The Test", "In the summer of sixteen twenty-eight, with the Vasa almost ready, Captain Söfring Hansson arranged a demonstration for Vice Admiral Klas Fleming. Thirty men were told to run from one side of the upper deck to the other, to see how much the ship would rock."),
 ("test", None, "After three runs, the ship was rolling so badly that Fleming stopped the test, afraid she would tip over. According to the ship's master, Fleming remarked that he wished the king were at home. But the king was away at war in Poland. And nobody stopped the launch."),

 ("harbour_sail", "Twenty Minutes", "On the tenth of August, sixteen twenty-eight, the Vasa left the quay below the royal palace, bound for the naval base at Älvsnabben. There was only a light breeze from the southwest. Hundreds, perhaps thousands, of people had come to watch. The guns were run out, and the gunports were open, ready to fire a salute."),
 ("harbour_sail", None, "Off Södermalm, a gust of wind hit the sails, and the Vasa heeled over to port. The crew let go of the sail ropes, and slowly, she came upright again. For a moment, it looked as if the danger had passed."),
 ("sinking", None, "Then, near Tegelviken, a stronger gust struck. The ship leaned over again, further this time. Water rushed in through the open lower gunports. Once the sea was pouring in, there was no saving her. The Vasa sank, about thirteen hundred metres from the quay, and only around a hundred and twenty metres from shore."),
 ("sinking", None, "She settled in thirty-two metres of water. Survivors clung to floating wreckage and to the tops of the masts, still sticking out above the waves. About thirty people are thought to have died. The pride of the Swedish navy had been afloat for roughly twenty minutes."),

 ("inquiry", "Who's to Blame?", "It took more than two weeks for the news to reach the king in Poland. He was furious, and demanded that the guilty be punished. In September, a tribunal of the Privy Council and the Admiralty met at the Royal Palace in Stockholm to find out what had gone wrong."),
 ("inquiry", None, "But the answers were awkward. The captain insisted the guns had been properly secured. The crew hadn't been drunk. The ship had been built to the dimensions the king had approved. When Arendt de Groote was asked why the ship had sunk, he reportedly answered: only God knows."),
 ("inquiry", None, "Hein Jacobsson testified that he had widened the ship by forty-three centimetres, but couldn't make her any wider. In the end, the easiest person to blame was the one who couldn't answer back. The late Master Henrik. Nobody was punished."),

 ("underwater", "333 Years in the Dark", "Within three days, a contract was signed to raise the wreck. An English engineer managed to pull the ship upright, but only pushed it deeper into the mud. In the sixteen sixties, salvagers used a diving bell to bring up more than fifty of the ship's valuable bronze guns. After that, the Vasa was largely forgotten."),
 ("underwater", None, "The diving bell was a simple but terrifying piece of technology. A heavy bell, open at the bottom, was lowered into the water with a diver standing inside, breathing the air trapped above his head. Working in near darkness and freezing cold, the salvagers hooked the heavy guns one by one and hauled them up. It was an extraordinary feat for the sixteen hundreds."),
 ("underwater", None, "Over the following centuries, people occasionally remembered the wreck. In eighteen forty-four, a navy officer claimed to have found it. Divers went down in the eighteen nineties. In nineteen twenty, someone applied for permission to salvage it, and was turned down. But nobody actually brought the Vasa home."),
 ("underwater", None, "But the Baltic had been kind to her. Its brackish water is hostile to the shipworm, a creature that devours wooden wrecks in saltier seas. In the nineteen fifties, an amateur archaeologist called Anders Franzén realised that old wrecks in Stockholm's waters might still be intact."),
 ("underwater", None, "In nineteen fifty-six, using a homemade probe to take samples from the seabed, Franzén hit something large and wooden near the Beckholmen dock. It was the Vasa."),
 ("raising", None, "Raising her was a huge job. Over two years, divers dug six tunnels underneath the hull, in pitch darkness and thick mud, to pass lifting cables beneath it. More than thirteen hundred dives were made without a serious accident. In nineteen fifty-nine, the ship was lifted in eighteen stages into shallower water."),
 ("raising", None, "And on the twenty-fourth of April, nineteen sixty-one, after three hundred and thirty-three years on the seabed, the Vasa broke the surface of Stockholm harbour once again."),

 ("raising", None, "Inside, the Vasa was a time capsule. Archaeologists recovered thousands of objects: spare clothes and shoes, tools, small copper coins, food the sailors had bought for themselves. They also found the remains of at least fifteen people, the crew who never made it off the ship."),
 ("raising", None, "Some parts couldn't be saved as they were. The high stern castle, with many of its sculptures, had collapsed into the mud over the centuries, and a temporary replacement had to be built before the lift. Divers and archaeologists then spent years recovering fallen carvings from the seabed, piece by piece, like a giant jigsaw puzzle."),
 ("museum", "What Remains", "Preserving her took decades. The waterlogged wood was sprayed with a chemical called polyethylene glycol for seventeen years, then very slowly dried. Since nineteen ninety, the Vasa has had her own museum in Stockholm, and more than forty-five million people have come to see her."),
 ("museum", None, "Standing next to her is a strange experience. She's enormous and dark, towering over the visitors, almost exactly as she looked on her first day at sea. Which, of course, was also her last. Every detail the king paid for is still there to see. The two gun decks. The carvings. The sheer ambition of it all."),
 ("museum", None, "She's considered the best-preserved seventeenth-century ship in the world. Six of her ten sails survived. So did many of her carvings, and thousands of everyday objects belonging to the people on board. Even today, the work to keep her intact continues, with new supports being built to hold the old hull in shape."),
 ("plans", None, "There's one more twist. When researchers measured the ship, they found rulers based on two different kinds of foot, Swedish and Amsterdam, which may help explain why the hull is a little heavier on one side. It's a tantalising clue, though the ship's main problem was simple. Too much weight, too high up."),
 ("museum", None, "Business schools even talk about a Vasa syndrome, when ambitious projects keep growing until they fail. Historians point out the real story is more complicated than that. But either way, Sweden's greatest warship ended up teaching the world a lesson. Just not the one the king had in mind."),
 ("outro", None, "That was the story of the Vasa. If you enjoyed it, subscribe to Blunderbuss History for the full stories of history's biggest blunders. See you in the next one."),
]
